; The target-independent front end (.ai/asm_plan.md §4.7): bytes of source text
; in, statements and token slices out.
;
; Split out of asm_core so that the two halves of the assembler can be linked
; apart. Everything downstream of a parsed line - the ASTs, lowering, encoding,
; layout, binding - needs none of this, and a program that builds instructions
; as values rather than reading them from a file should not carry a lexer and a
; Menhir table it never calls. test/snippets is exactly that program, and
; test/oracle's conformance driver reaches the snippets through it.
;
; The cut is the one the module graph already had: nothing here is referenced by
; asm_core, and everything here is rooted at Token. asm_core keeps the
; containers - expr, perms, directive, and the three staged ASTs.
;
; Menhir owns the common statement and expression grammar (§4.7) and nothing
; else. Individual instruction forms must never become Menhir productions, so
; operands leave the grammar as token slices.

(library
 (name asm_syntax)
 (modes byte native)
 (libraries foundation asm_core fmt)
 (enabled_if
  (<> %{env:ASM_MELANGE=false} true)))

; Menhir is invoked by an explicit rule rather than by the (menhir ...) stanza,
; because that stanza takes no enabled_if and needs a library to attach to - and
; this library is disabled in the Melange configuration, where the mirror under
; melange/lib/asm_syntax owns the build instead. Exactly one of the two rules is
; ever enabled, so grammar.ml has exactly one producer and both configurations
; compile a parser generated from this one .mly.

(rule
 (targets grammar.ml grammar.mli)
 (deps grammar.mly)
 (enabled_if
  (<> %{env:ASM_MELANGE=false} true))
 (action
  (run menhir --base grammar %{deps})))
